{
  "an10.18:0.1": "Numbered Discourses 10.18 ",
  "an10.18:0.2": "2. A Protector ",
  "an10.18:0.3": "A Protector (2nd) ",
  "an10.18:1.1": "So I have heard. ",
  "an10.18:1.2": "At one time the Buddha was staying near Sāvatthī in Jeta’s Grove, Anāthapiṇḍika’s monastery. ",
  "an10.18:1.3": "There the Buddha addressed the mendicants, ",
  "an10.18:1.4": "“Mendicants!” ",
  "an10.18:1.5": "“Venerable sir,” they replied. ",
  "an10.18:1.6": "The Buddha said this: ",
  "an10.18:2.1": "“Mendicants, you should live with a protector, not without one. ",
  "an10.18:2.2": "Living without a protector is suffering. ",
  "an10.18:2.3": "There are ten qualities that serve as protector. ",
  "an10.18:2.4": "What ten? ",
  "an10.18:2.5": "Firstly, a mendicant is ethical, restrained in the monastic code, conducting themselves well and resorting for alms in suitable places. Seeing danger in the slightest fault, they keep the rules they’ve undertaken. ",
  "an10.18:2.6": "Knowing this, the mendicants—whether senior, middle, or junior—think that mendicant is worth advising and instructing. ",
  "an10.18:2.7": "Being treated with such kindness by the senior, middle, and junior mendicants, that mendicant can expect only growth, not decline. ",
  "an10.18:2.8": "This is a quality that serves as protector. ",
  "an10.18:3.1": "Furthermore, a mendicant is very learned, remembering and keeping what they’ve learned. These teachings are good in the beginning, good in the middle, and good in the end, meaning